  "answer": "### Short Answer\nSpouses who both earn compensation income cannot file a joint income tax return; they must file separate returns based on their respective total taxable income.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nUnder Section 24 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), married individuals are required to compute their income tax separately based on their respective total taxable income.\n\n### Interpretation\nThe NIRC explicitly states that for married individuals, each spouse must compute their income tax separately unless income cannot be definitively attributed to either spouse, in which case it shall be divided equally for tax purposes. This provision ensures that each spouse's income is assessed independently, reflecting their individual earnings and tax liabilities.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nIn practice, this means that each spouse must file their own income tax return, detailing their respective compensation income. This requirement can affect tax planning strategies, as the combined income may push them into a higher tax bracket if considered together, but they must still adhere to the separate filing requirement as mandated by law.",
